+Maintains a consistent, multi-resolution tetrahedral mesh using a graph cut through a DAG of hierarchical segments. It guarantees crack-free topology and enables efficient out-of-core processing, differing from Active Cut Management's octree-based texel adaptation.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The adaptation phase is bounded by the frame time budget. The number of merges and splits is limited by bandwidth (disk/RAM/texture) and cache hit rates [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Dependent on the number of active nodes and their texture residency. Texture memory usage increases with splits and decreases with merges [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Maintains texel sizes close to one pixel, optimizing fill rate and visual quality. Tested on an Oxygen Post model with 513,375 tetrahedra [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** View-dependent refinement ensures higher detail near the viewpoint. Dynamic adaptation handles varying scene complexity and camera motion.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful management of texture memory and bandwidth. Split operations may not complete in a single frame, requiring state persistence.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of nodes in the active cut and the cost of priority queue operations. The number of merges/splits is bounded by the frame time budget and bandwidth [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of nodes in the active cut and the texture memory required for their data [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Tested on the Oxygen Post model (513,375 tetrahedra) [S1]. Performance is influenced by fill rate, bandwidth (disk/RAM/texture), and cache hit rates [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** View-dependent refinement ensures high quality near the viewpoint; adapts to dynamic scenes; manages memory bandwidth explicitly [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires frequent data loading (RAM/disk) for splits; complex priority management; sensitive to cache misses [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Interactive visualization of large unstructured grids where view-dependent detail is critical and hardware bandwidth is a bottleneck [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Static scenes with no need for dynamic LOD; hardware with very limited texture memory or no disk-to-RAM bandwidth optimization [G].
 
 ## Background
-Active Cut Management was introduced in the context of interactive visualization of unstructured grids using hierarchical 3D representations [S1]. It draws conceptual parallels to view-dependent continuous level of detail techniques used for polygonal models, such as those by Xia and Varshney, Duchandeau et al., Hoppe, and Luebke and Erikson [S1]. The method was demonstrated on datasets like the Oxygen Post model (513,375 tetrahedra) and Cylinder data set (615,000 tetrahedra) [S1].
+Active Cut Management was introduced by Leven et al. in the context of interactive visualization of unstructured grids using hierarchical 3D structures [S1]. It builds on the concept of the "active cut" from view-dependent continuous level of detail for polygonal models, such as those by Xia and Varshney (1996), Duchaideau et al. (1997), Hoppe (1997), and Luebke and Erikson (1997) [S1]. The method was designed to handle the specific challenges of volume rendering, including texture memory constraints and the need for real-time adaptation [S1].
 
